Build the pthread kext into the kernel instead of loading it.

xnu has no pthread implementation of its own. bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c is a
table of function pointers and nothing more, and the only thing that ever fills
it is com.apple.kec.pthread -- the kext half of Apple's libpthread. A kernel
built from this tree alone therefore cannot create a user thread at all:
libpthread's runtime calls bsdthread_register() during the startup of every
process, and that call goes straight through the table.

There are no kexts in this port, so the kext is compiled into the kernel.

Three things make this smaller than it sounds. xnu-4903 already carries the
workqueue engine itself -- bsd/conf/files lists pthread_workqueue.c and
pthread_priority.c as standard -- so what remains in the kext is the psynch
locks, bsdthread_*, and registration: three sources, about 4200 lines.
Compatibility is structural rather than negotiated, because the kext includes
<sys/pthread_shims.h> from xnu, so the table is defined in exactly one place
and the static_asserts at the top of pthread_shims.c check both halves against
it. And the start routine is three lines:

    kern_return_t pthread_start(kmod_info_t *ki, void *d)
    {
            pthread_kext_register((pthread_functions_t)&pthread_internal_functions,
                &pthread_kern);
            return KERN_SUCCESS;
    }

Nothing calls it when the code is linked in, so pthread_init() calls it by hand
the first time the table is needed. The call is guarded on the table still
being NULL, so it happens once; pthread_kext_register() panics on a second
registration.

The sources are staged into bsd/pthread/kext by the build script, which fetches
libpthread-330.250.2 -- the 10.14.6 release train, the same one xnu-4903.270.47
comes from. They keep libpthread's own directory layout, because they include
each other by project-relative path ("kern/synch_internal.h", "pthread/qos.h",
"private/qos_private.h"), which is why the three objects get an -I pointing at
the staging root. The angle-bracket includes -- <pthread/bsdthread_private.h>,
<pthread/priority_private.h>, <pthread/workqueue_syscalls.h> -- are xnu's own
and resolve against the installed headers, so they are not staged.

Two of the three sources are renamed on the way in:

    kern/kern_synch.c    -> kern/pthread_synch.c
    kern/kern_support.c  -> kern/pthread_support.c

kern_synch.c would collide with xnu's own bsd/kern/kern_synch.c, because the
conf system names objects after the basename alone. The new names are not
arbitrary: they are the ones Apple used while this code still lived in xnu, and
bsd/conf/Makefile.template in this very tree still carries

    pthread_synch.o_CFLAGS_ADD   += -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
    pthread_support.o_CFLAGS_ADD += -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes

for two files that no longer exist. Renaming to match brings those suppressions
back to the code they were written for; pthread_kext.c gets the same pair.

This patch needs pthread-guards-without-kext.patch, which applies before it and
leaves pthread_init() able to survive a NULL table. The two are deliberately
separate and independently removable: the guards are what keeps the boot alive
if the registration ever does not happen, and if this integration has to be
backed out the guards stay behind on their own.

How to tell whether it worked: with the kext registered, pthread_init() prints
nothing. The line

    pthread: kernel extension not loaded; pthread and workqueue syscalls will
    return ENOTSUP

appearing at boot means the table is still NULL and only the guards are holding
the kernel up.

This cannot be tested beyond that until there is a root filesystem. Nothing in
the kernel calls into the table on its own; the first real exercise of it is a
userland process registering its thread start routines.

--- a/bsd/conf/files
+++ b/bsd/conf/files
@@ -385,6 +385,9 @@
 bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c		standard
 bsd/pthread/pthread_priority.c		standard
 bsd/pthread/pthread_workqueue.c		standard
+bsd/pthread/kext/kern/pthread_kext.c	standard
+bsd/pthread/kext/kern/pthread_support.c	standard
+bsd/pthread/kext/kern/pthread_synch.c	standard
 
 bsd/kern/bsd_init.c			standard
 ./init_sysent.c				standard
--- a/bsd/conf/Makefile.template
+++ b/bsd/conf/Makefile.template
@@ -83,6 +83,14 @@
 pthread_synch.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
 pthread_support.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
 
+# The pthread kext sources staged under bsd/pthread/kext keep libpthread's
+# own layout, because they include each other by project-relative path
+# ("kern/synch_internal.h", "pthread/qos.h", "private/qos_private.h").
+pthread_kext.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -I$(SRCROOT)/bsd/pthread/kext
+pthread_support.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -I$(SRCROOT)/bsd/pthread/kext
+pthread_synch.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -I$(SRCROOT)/bsd/pthread/kext
+pthread_kext.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
+
 # Objects that don't want -Wsign-compare
 OBJS_NO_SIGN_COMPARE =		\
 		radix.o	\
--- a/bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c
+++ b/bsd/pthread/pthread_shims.c
@@ -53,6 +53,14 @@
 #include <vm/vm_map.h>
 #include <vm/vm_protos.h>
 #include <kern/kcdata.h>
+#include <mach/kmod.h>
+
+/*
+ * com.apple.kec.pthread's start routine. This port has no kexts, so the kext
+ * is compiled into the kernel (bsd/pthread/kext, staged by the build script)
+ * and its start routine is called by hand from pthread_init() below.
+ */
+extern kern_return_t pthread_start(kmod_info_t *ki, void *d);
 
 /* version number of the in-kernel shims given to pthread.kext */
 #define PTHREAD_SHIMS_VERSION 1
@@ -321,6 +329,14 @@
 void
 pthread_init(void)
 {
+	/*
+	 * Nothing loads the kext, so nothing has called pthread_kext_register()
+	 * yet. Do it here, once, immediately before the table is first needed.
+	 */
+	if (PTHREAD_KEXT_ABSENT()) {
+		(void)pthread_start(NULL, NULL);
+	}
+
 	if (PTHREAD_KEXT_ABSENT()) {
 		printf("pthread: kernel extension not loaded; pthread and "
 		    "workqueue syscalls will return ENOTSUP\n");
